+# Pickup reminders
+
+For whoever changes how members are reminded of a pickup, or proposes sending the reminders by email to save the SMS
+cost. The day before a pickup, larder texts the member that their pantry order is ready. Reminders go by SMS because
+from April to June 2025, when they went by email, missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders.
+
+## Message
+
+The day before the pickup slot, `remind` in `larder/reminders.py` sends the member one message: "Your pantry order is
+ready for pickup" followed by the slot.
+
+## Channel
+
+The message goes by SMS to the member's phone, `CHANNEL = "sms"` in `larder/reminders.py`. Setting it to anything else
+sends it to the member's email address instead.
+
+SMS costs about 40 euros a month, and email would cost nothing. Volunteers have proposed email several times for that
+reason; [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md) was the third time in a year. It was tried from April to June 2025:
+
+- The email provider's report showed 34% of the reminder emails were never opened.
+- Missed pickups rose from 6% of pantry orders to 13%. Each missed pickup is a packed bag, and its fresh food is
+  thrown away at closing time.
+- Many members share one phone across their family and never check email, but they see an SMS.
+
+Reminders went back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and by August missed pickups were back down to 6%. The
+maintainers kept SMS and closed issue 52.
